WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.830
With over a billion potential movie fans,
China has quickly become Hollywood's most

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:09.280
coveted demographic, which explains why studios
are bending over backwards to appeal to Chinese

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.420
audiences and appease Chinese government censors.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.300
It happens all the time, but here's a look
at some of the most blatant examples of movies

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:19.349
Hollywood was forced to change for China.

00:00:19.349 --> 00:00:20.919
Star Trek Beyond

00:00:20.919 --> 00:00:25.900
After J.J. Abrams departed the Star Trek franchise,
Taiwanese director Justin Lin was brought

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.130
in to give Star Trek Beyond some of the kick
he had previously provided to four Fast and

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.140
Furious films.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.910
During the Asian press tour for Star Trek
Beyond, Lin revealed that he tailor made the

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.110
film to appeal to Chinese audiences specifically,
interviewing Chinese film fans to see what

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.110
they liked.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.860
"I talked to a lot of people on why they go
to movies, what kind of movies they want to

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:45.860
see.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.930
And the thing that I got back was that everyone
here, such great people and they work so hard,

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:55.480
and they want to get a sense of like to escape
and to really kind of just enjoy, have fun,

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.990
laugh and enjoy big action, and I feel like
this movie is tailor-made for the Chinese

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.400
audience."

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.930
Midnight Cowboy

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:08.340
If there's one thing Chinese censors really
don't like, it's LGBTQ characters and themes.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.469
For decades, the government has cracked down
on everything gay, leading to some films being

00:01:12.469 --> 00:01:13.970
butchered beyond comprehension.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:19.710
Case in point: the 1969 classic Midnight Cowboy,
which one Chinese-Malaysian film buff described

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:25.070
in Anthropologica as a confusing mess when
he saw a censored version in the 1970's.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:28.850
"The first thing about homosexuality that
I remember making an impact on me was the

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.380
movie Midnight Cowboy.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.869
It made me more confused than ever.

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:35.649
I didn't speak English and trying to read
the subtitles in Chinese, I didn't know what

00:01:35.649 --> 00:01:36.649
was going on.

00:01:36.649 --> 00:01:39.740
It was very badly edited for Chinese audiences."

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.049
​X-Men: Days of Future Past

00:01:42.049 --> 00:01:47.070
When it comes to tokenism, Chinese superstar
Fan Bingbing has had more than her share of

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.270
problems, particularly with superhero films.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:53.729
After being signed up for Iron Man 3, it turned
out her role was for a scene that only appeared

00:01:53.729 --> 00:01:54.960
in the Chinese release.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.670
And her experience on X-Men: Days of Future
Past was almost as bad.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:03.389
Bingbing signed a contract to appear in four
X-Men films, but instead, her character Blink

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:06.369
has been in just one, with barely any dialogue
to show for it.

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:11.489
Bingbing told The Star that she had her suspicions
when director Bryan Singer contacted her about

00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:12.489
the role.

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:16.489
"In the comics, Blink is a Caucasian girl,
so I was curious why Bryan [Singer] wanted

00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:18.209
an Asian to play the role."

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:20.650
Unfortunately, we all know the answer.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.590
Transformers: Age of Extinction

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:27.080
The producers behind Transformers: Age of
Extinction went all out to pander to Chinese

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.080
audiences.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.260
Not only were some of the film's biggest moments
filmed in China, the movie's world premiere

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.599
was held in Hong Kong.

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:38.150
Plus, it's jam packed with product placement,
some of which doesn't even make sense, like

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.099
Chinese ATMs in Texas.

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:44.640
But hey, of all the things that make no sense
about the Transformers films, that's not even

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.860
in the top fifty.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.150
​The Karate Kid

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:52.459
This remake of the classic '80s Karate Kid
changed essentially everything about the original

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:54.060
in order to appeal to Chinese audiences.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:59.099
In fact, it's not even about karate at all,
as the updated version features Jaden Smith

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:03.720
moving to China, where he learns the finer
points of the Chinese art of kung fu from

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.500
Hong Kong legend Jackie Chan.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.640
The pandering was bad enough that The Guardian
suggested it should come with disclaimer that

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:13.599
the film was brought to you by the "China
National Tourist Office".

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:15.409
Sounds about right.

00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:16.650
Logan

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:21.890
Given how hyper violent the superhero blockbuster
Logan was, it's no surprise that censors ended

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:26.670
up pressuring Fox into cutting a whopping
14 minutes from the film's run time.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.400
What's surprising is that they didn't demand
even more cuts.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.280
In fact, Logan was the first film released
in China under a new, less restrictive law

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:39.260
that introduced the concept of age-based film
ratings, with fans being warned in advance

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:43.380
that "'elementary school students and pre-school
children must be accompanied by parents or

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.380
guardians."

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:49.870
Essentially, China got a PG rated version
of Logan, rather a G rated version.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.400
Baby steps!

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:52.700
Cloud Atlas

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.519
Audiences all over the world were perplexed
by the convoluted and complex sci-fi opus

00:03:57.519 --> 00:04:02.870
Cloud Atlas, which wove multiple storylines
in multiple time periods together to form

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.470
a larger epic narrative.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:09.030
But film fans trying to figure out the plot
in China faced an even more daunting challenge,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:14.760
as censors cut an incredible 40 minutes from
the film, including an entire storyline involving

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.340
a same sex romance between in 1930's Britain.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.210
The film's three directors, Tom Tykwer and
Lana and Lilly Wachowski, claimed to be totally

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:26.620
fine with the changes, telling reporters in
China that they trusted their Chinese distributors

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.050
to get the edits right.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:32.500
But if you really want to understand the story,
you might be better off just reading the book.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.639
Independence Day: Resurgence

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:39.039
Since the original Independence Day was never
released in China, Fox had to go a different

00:04:39.039 --> 00:04:41.740
route in drumming up interest in 2016's sequel.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:46.340
They started by writing a story where America
teams up with China to save the world, and

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:51.250
followed that up by casting Chinese pop star
Angelababy in a minor role and then heavily

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:53.919
hyping her bit part in promotional material.

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:58.510
Chinese audiences found that annoying, but
not as bad as some of the blatant product

00:04:58.510 --> 00:04:59.510
placement.

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:04.100
Not only does Liam Hemsworth's American pilot
inexplicably use Chinese instant messaging

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.100
service QQ…

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.940
"Give them a little credit, Jake…"

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.980
...but a Chinese drink called Moon Milk features
prominently, leaving perplexed viewers venting

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:17.680
their frustrations online.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.419
One Weibo user sarcastically posted, "When
getting revenge on evil aliens, everyone should

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:25.250
take two boxes of MengNiu Moon Milk with them."

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:26.500
Thanks for watching!

